---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const qrPath = `${base}qr/2026-08-02-altar-undivided-heart.png`;
const pageURL = 'https://dmicalambaworship.github.io/teachings/2026-08-02-altar-undivided-heart/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Altar of the Heart: An Undivided Heart for God | DMI Calamba City"
  description="A concise Bible study from Psalm 86:11 about surrender, undivided allegiance, and a life offered fully to God."
  bodyClass="study-page"
>
  <header class="study-hero">
    <nav class="study-nav" aria-label="Primary navigation">
      <a href={base} aria-label="Return to DMI Calamba City home">DMI · Calamba City</a>
      <span>02 August 2026</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="study-hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Bible Study · Psalm 86:11</p>
      <h1>Altar of the Heart</h1>
      <p class="study-subtitle">An Undivided Heart for God</p>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="hero-scripture">
      <p>“Teach me your way, O Lord, that I may walk in your truth; unite my heart to fear your name.”</p>
      <cite>Psalm 86:11 ESV</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </header>

  <main class="study-content">
    <aside class="study-aside" aria-label="Study summary">
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label">Theme</span>
        <p>God gathers every scattered desire around himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label">Read</span>
        <p>Psalm 86:10–11<br />Ezekiel 14:3–6<br />Romans 12:1–2</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label">Time</span>
        <p>20–25 minutes</p>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <article class="study-article">
      <section class="opening" aria-labelledby="opening-title">
        <p class="section-number">01 · Begin here</p>
        <h2 id="opening-title">What has the final word?</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          David does not merely ask for stronger feelings. He asks God to make his inner life one. The problem is often not that we have no desire for God, but that another desire has gained <strong>veto power over our obedience</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          An idol is more than something we enjoy. It is anything created that receives the trust, fear, allegiance, or obedience that belongs ultimately to God.
        </p>
        <div class="key-statement">
          <span>Key truth</span>
          <p>An undivided heart is not an empty heart. It is a heart whose loves, choices, and ambitions converge on God.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="movement-one">
        <p class="section-number">02 · Receive</p>
        <h2 id="movement-one">Let God unite the heart</h2>
        <p>
          Psalm 86:11 moves in a clear order: <strong>teach me</strong>, so that I may <strong>walk</strong>, and <strong>unite my heart</strong>. David begins with God’s way, not a path he has already selected. Truth is not simply information to admire; it becomes a life of obedience.
        </p>
        <p>
          “Unite my heart” is a prayer for an integrated inner life—thought, will, affection, and intention gathered around reverence for God. Verse 10 gives the reason: <strong>“You alone are God.”</strong> No competing master deserves the throne.
        </p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span>Discuss · 1</span>
          <p>Where is my heart divided between God’s way and another way?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="movement-two">
        <p class="section-number">03 · Release</p>
        <h2 id="movement-two">Clear the altar of rival loves</h2>
        <p>
          In Ezekiel 14, the elders sit before God’s prophet while carrying idols within. Their posture looks spiritual, but their allegiance is already decided. They want guidance without surrender.
        </p>
        <p>
          God’s invitation is direct: <strong>repent and turn away</strong>. Repentance is not self-hatred; it is the end of defending what God is confronting. A contrite heart can finally say, “Jesus, this cannot be my lord. You alone are God.”
        </p>
        <ul class="diagnostic-list" aria-label="Questions for recognizing an idol">
          <li>What do I fear losing most?</li>
          <li>Which outcome am I demanding from God?</li>
          <li>Whose approval controls my obedience?</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span>Discuss · 2</span>
          <p>What am I asking God to bless that he may be asking me to surrender?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="movement-three">
        <p class="section-number">04 · Respond</p>
        <h2 id="movement-three">Place the whole life before God</h2>
        <p>
          “The altar of the heart” is a biblical picture, not a phrase quoted directly from Scripture. Romans 12 gives the response behind the picture: present your bodies as a living sacrifice. Worship is not one religious moment; it is a whole life continually offered to God.
        </p>
        <p>
          Prayer keeps returning the scattered heart to its center. Scripture reshapes our desires, repentance removes what competes, obedience walks in truth, and surrender leaves nothing withheld. These practices do not earn God’s presence; they keep us responsive to grace.
        </p>
        <div class="key-statement dark">
          <span>Remember</span>
          <p>Grace supplies what we cannot manufacture. Our response is to remain teachable, repent quickly, pray continually, and obey wholeheartedly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span>Discuss · 3</span>
          <p>What one daily rhythm would help me return my whole heart to God?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="response-section" aria-labelledby="response-title">
        <p class="section-number">05 · A simple response</p>
        <h2 id="response-title">Notice. Surrender. Return.</h2>
        <ol class="response-steps">
          <li>
            <span>Notice</span>
            <p>Name the fear, desire, resentment, or compromise dividing your allegiance.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>Surrender</span>
            <p>Open your hands and place your plans, reputation, relationships, resources, and future before God.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>Return</span>
            <p>Choose one concrete rhythm: Scripture before your phone, a midday prayer pause, or an evening heart review.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="closing-prayer" aria-labelledby="prayer-title">
        <p class="section-number">Pray together</p>
        <h2 id="prayer-title">A prayer for an undivided heart</h2>
        <p>
          Father, teach us your way and cause us to walk in your truth. Gather every scattered affection, ambition, fear, and desire. Expose the idols we carry into your presence, and free us from pride and self-defense. By your Spirit, teach us to repent without delay, pray without ceasing, and offer our whole lives to you. Jesus, you alone are God. Unite our hearts to fear your name. Amen.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="takeaway" aria-label="Study takeaway">
        <span>Take this with you</span>
        <p>An undivided heart is continually taught by God’s Word, cleared through repentance, centered through prayer, and offered through surrender.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="qr-section" aria-labelledby="share-title">
        <div>
          <p class="section-number">Share the study</p>
          <h2 id="share-title">Open on another device</h2>
          <p>Scan or download this QR code to return directly to this Bible study.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href={qrPath} download="dmi-altar-of-the-heart-qr.png">Download QR code</a>
        </div>
        <a class="qr-card" href={pageURL} aria-label="Open this Bible study">
          <img src={qrPath} width="720" height="720" alt="QR code for Altar of the Heart Bible study" />
        </a>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <a href={base}>DMI Calamba City</a>
    <span>Gathered around Jesus. Formed by his Word.</span>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
